'''''Lithops schwantesii''''' is a [[Succulent plant|succulent]] plant of the genus ''[[Lithops]]'' and a member of the family [[Aizoaceae]]. ''L. schwantesii'' receives its name from [[Martin Heinrich Gustav Schwantes|Gustav Schwantes]], a prominent archaeologist and botanist of late 19th and 20th century. The succulent also received the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]] for being a resilient and easy to cultivate plant.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/10373/i-Lithops-schwantesii-i/Details|title=RHS - Lithops schwantesii|last=|first=|date=|website=Royal Horticultural Society|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=}}</ref>

== Description ==
[[File:Collage Lithops schwantesii.jpg|thumb|''Lithops schwantesii''|alt=|left]]
This particular species, like that of all in its genus, grows best in direct sunlight. It originates and grows in South Africa. The plant has channels that run along the tops of its leaves. The channels are irregular, and do not usually run in a common pattern. Leaves are usually grey with tinges of red, blue pink, brown, or green. Its flowers are small-medium in size and yellow in color.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.floweringstones.co.za/species/schwantesii.html|title=Lithops schwantesii|website=www.floweringstones.co.za|access-date=2019-03-13}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.llifle.com/Encyclopedia/SUCCULENTS/Family/Aizoaceae/11518/Lithops_schwantesii|title=Lithops schwantesii|website=www.llifle.com|access-date=2019-03-13}}</ref>
